\section{100-Point Continuous Likert Scale}
\label{app:likert100}

To test whether the Blindspot reflects integer-scale granularity rather than a paradigm difference between absolute and relative evaluation, we replicate the formality and verbosity experiments using a 100-point continuous scale ($0$--$100$) with Qwen3-32B as judge (Table~\ref{tab:likert100}).
Both axes remain non-significant: formality $p = 0.725$ (tie rate ${\sim}66\%$) and verbosity $p = 0.117$ (tie rate ${\sim}52\%$), while the same data yield BT $66.5\%$ ($p < 0.0001$) and $77.3\%$ ($p < 0.0001$) respectively.
The persistence of high tie rates even with 101 possible values confirms that LLM judges do not exploit finer granularity to express style preferences; the Blindspot arises from absolute-vs-relative evaluation paradigms, not scale resolution.

